.TH CHTTP_REQUEST_NEW_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_request_new_mp \- allocate and initialize an HTTP request with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

chttp_request_t *chttp_request_new_mp(chttp_method_t method,
                                      const char *url,
                                      const chttp_request_body_t *body,
                                      ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                                      char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function allocates a new chttp_request_t. It copies the URL into
a buffer that the request owns. It copies the body data and the
content_type string in the same way if they are not NULL. The caller
can free its own originals at once after this call returns.

Give NULL for body, or use the CHTTP_NO_BODY macro, when the request
needs no body. GET and DELETE are such requests. body->data can be
NULL only when body->len == 0. The function rejects a NULL body->data
with a body->len that is not zero, and gives NULL. It does not treat
that pair as a request with no body.

Only CHTTP_POST, CHTTP_PUT and CHTTP_PATCH send a body. For a request
with a different method, the client never sends the body. The client
also sends no Content-Length and no Content-Type for that request. A
POST, PUT or PATCH with no body goes out with "Content-Length: 0".

The expect_continue field of the new request defaults to false,
because the library fills the struct with zero. Set the field to true
after construction, for example with req->expect_continue = true. The
client then sends "Expect: 100-continue". It waits for the interim
response of the server before it sends the body. See chttpclient_do(3)
for the full protocol. The field has no effect on a request with no
body. It also has no effect on a request that already has an explicit
"Expect" header from chttp_request_set_header. Every tier honors this
field in the same way: chttpclient_do and chttpclient_do_streaming,
chttpclient_do_async and chttpclient_do_async_streaming, and
chttpclient_do_pooled and chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
method    The HTTP method (CHTTP_GET, CHTTP_POST and the others).
url       The target URL. It must not be NULL. The function copies it
          into the request.
body      A pointer to a chttp_request_body_t, or NULL for no body.
mprocs    A custom allocator, or NULL for malloc/free.
err_str   Optional. It gets a static error string if the call fails.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to the new request, or NULL if the call fails. The call
fails for a NULL url, for a NULL body->data with a body->len that is
not zero, and for a failure of an allocation.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Every allocation uses mprocs when mprocs is not NULL.
The request must stay alive until every chttpclient_do call that uses
it returns. Such a synchronous call reads the method, the body and the
headers of the request directly.

The function copies body->content_type here without a change and
without a check. It cannot know yet whether that value ever reaches
the wire. An explicit "content-type" header that you set later with
chttp_request_set_header wins over it and stops it. Does
body->content_type still hold a CR byte or an LF byte when the library
serializes the request? The chttpclient_do call, the chttp_run_query
call or the other call that uses this request then fails with
ccol_invalid_args. The library does not write that value onto the
wire. See chttp_request_set_header(3) for the reason.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttp_request_new (3),
.BR chttp_request_set_header (3),
.BR chttp_request_free (3),
.BR chttpclient_do (3)
